//! The Image module: BMP files, surfaces and GL textures for `Simp`.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.5's image-decode item, and the last graphics-shaped one — the remaining §8.5 entry
//! is audio, which is not.
//!
//! # Why BMP and not PNG
//!
//! **SDL2 decodes BMP itself.** `SDL_LoadBMP_RW` is in the base library, so this module needs no second
//! dependency on top of the one ADR-0163 §1 already accepted as a stated cost. PNG needs `SDL_image`, which is
//! a separate library with its own version skew — and a decoder written in Jairs needs zlib's inflate, which is
//! a real project and not an item inside a graphics wave.
//!
//! So this is the honest scope: **loading an image works, and the format is the one that costs nothing**. A
//! caller who needs PNG binds `SDL_image` the same way `Window` binds SDL2, and this module is the shape to
//! copy.
//!
//! **Rejected: writing a PNG decoder here.** It would be the largest single thing in the standard library, and
//! it belongs beside a `Compress` module rather than inside `Image`.
//!
//! **Rejected: skipping images entirely** and calling §8.5's item deferred. A texture path that has never
//! carried a real decoded image is a texture path nobody has tested — and the decode is the part with the
//! interesting failure (a missing file, a corrupt header), which is exactly what a caller needs a flag for.
//!
//! # Surface, then texture, and why both exist
//!
//! A **surface** is pixels in ordinary memory. A **texture** is pixels the renderer can draw from, which is
//! now always pixels on the GPU: `Simp` has one backend, OpenGL (ADR-0187 §2), and there is no other kind of
//! texture to produce. Loading produces a surface; drawing needs a texture; so a caller does both and frees
//! the surface. That is SDL's shape and this module does not hide it, because hiding it would mean either
//! keeping every surface alive forever or deciding for the caller when to convert.
//!
//! `load_texture` does the whole sequence for the common case, and `load_bmp` plus `texture_from` are there for a
//! caller who wants the pixels.
//!
//! # A texture upload needs a live GL context
//!
//! `texture_from` and `load_texture` call straight into `modules/GL`, which is undefined without a context
//! current on the calling thread (`GL`'s own docs say so). `Simp.set_render_target` is what creates one, so a
//! caller must have called that at least once before either of these — exactly the order every graphics
//! program already establishes before it draws anything.
//!
//! # Format conversion, and the byte-order trap in SDL's own naming
//!
//! `SDL_LoadBMP` gives back whatever format the file holds — a 24-bit BMP decodes to 24-bit BGR pixels, not
//! RGBA — and `GL.upload_texture_rgba` wants 32-bit RGBA, tightly packed, **bytes in memory in the order
//! R, G, B, A**: that is what `glTexImage2D(format = GL_RGBA, type = GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE)` reads. So
//! `texture_from` reads the loaded surface's own `SDL_PixelFormat` and, when it does not already match
//! `GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT`, hands the surface to `SDL_ConvertSurfaceFormat` before uploading anything. SDL owns
//! the channel-order and bit-depth tables for every format it can decode; reshuffling bytes by hand here
//! would be re-deriving what SDL already got right, for every format this module has never been tested
//! against. **A failed conversion fails the load** — this module never uploads pixels it has not made a
//! matching byte array first.
//!
//! **`SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888` is not that byte array**, on this machine or on any little-endian target
//! this library claims — and macOS, Linux and Windows on x86 and ARM64 are all little-endian, the same
//! fact `modules/GL`'s own per-OS split never has to branch on because none of the three differs on it.
//! SDL's *packed* format names describe a 32-bit integer's bit layout most-significant-byte first, and a
//! little-endian host stores that integer's bytes in the opposite order, so `RGBA8888`'s bytes in memory
//! are **A, B, G, R** — reversed. SDL's header states the fix directly: `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA32`, its own
//! "a byte array of R, G, B, A on the current platform" alias, is defined as `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_ABGR8888` on
//! a little-endian host and `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888` only on a big-endian one. `GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT` below
//! is that alias's value, **measured** by compiling three lines of C against the installed
//! `SDL_pixels.h` — `376840196`, `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_ABGR8888` — not the tempting-but-wrong literal
//! `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888` (`373694468`), which would have every upload read each pixel's channels
//! reversed: a pixel drawn solid red would upload as solid blue with the wrong alpha, silently. This
//! library has no endianness axis — `os()` is the only per-host value (ADR-0180) — so the value is
//! hardcoded for the little-endian hosts every target here is.
//!
//! # Pitch, and why `GL.set_unpack_alignment` is never called
//!
//! SDL pads a surface's rows for alignment, so a row's `pitch` is not always `width * 4` even for a 32-bit
//! surface. `GL.set_unpack_alignment`'s own doc names the failure this causes — a *3*-byte-per-pixel row
//! whose width is not a multiple of 4 — but this module never uploads 3 bytes per pixel: every upload here is
//! RGBA, 4 bytes per pixel, and `width * 4` is always a multiple of GL's default 4-byte alignment. So the
//! alignment is never wrong; what can still be wrong is the *source* row being longer than `width * 4`. When a
//! converted surface's `pitch` disagrees with `width * 4`, `texture_from` **repacks** the rows into a
//! tightly-packed scratch buffer from temporary storage before uploading, rather than either trusting the gap
//! away or calling an alignment knob that cannot fix a source stride. Measured on this machine: a
//! `create_surface`-built surface's `pitch` already equals `width * 4`, so the repack path exists for
//! a converted surface this module has not seen, not for the one it built itself.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No public pixel access.** `pixels` is exposed as a `*u8` on `Surface_Data` and no exported procedure
//! hands a caller its bytes — `texture_from` reads them internally only to repack a padded row, never to
//! interpret a colour. A caller who wants to poke pixels wants a whole format-conversion API rather than one
//! accessor.
//!
//! **No drawing.** This module used to draw through `Window`'s renderer directly; it no longer does, because
//! `Simp` is the one module that draws (ADR-0182 §4). A caller draws a loaded texture with
//! `Simp.set_shader_for_images` and `Simp.immediate_quad`/`immediate_quad_corners`, which is also the only way
//! to draw a texture at four arbitrary corners — the old `draw_texture` copied to an axis-aligned rectangle and
//! could not express that at all.
//!
//! **No sub-rectangle source.** Nothing here crops before uploading. A sprite sheet wants the whole texture
//! uploaded once and then a UV sub-rectangle at draw time, which is `Simp.immediate_quad_corners`'s job, not
//! this module's.

Window :: #import "Window";
Simp :: #import "Simp";
GL :: #import "GL";
#import "Basic";

// Imported **aliased** (ADR-0179 §1). `Window.Rect` is a real spelling now — it was not when this module was
// written, and this comment used to say so: *"an `#import` is flat and there is no `Window.Rect` syntax"*.
//
// **The prefixes stay anyway** — `fill_surface` and not `fill`, `free_surface` and not `free`,
// `destroy_texture` and not `destroy`. They were written unprefixed first, and a file importing `Window`,
// `Basic` and this module got **four E0211 ambiguous-name errors** at once. A qualified import makes that
// survivable; it does not make a short exported name a good idea, because a name this module exports is a
// claim on every importer that spells it bare (ADR-0167 §7).
//
// `Basic` stays a **bare** import — `talloc` is the one thing this module needs from it, for the row-repack
// scratch buffer, and a bare import is what every other private-`talloc` module here already does
// (`modules/GL`, `modules/Window`).

/// An `SDL_PixelFormat`, overlaid for the one field this module reads.
///
/// SDL's real struct is `{Uint32 format; SDL_Palette *palette; Uint8 BitsPerPixel; Uint8 BytesPerPixel;
/// Uint8 padding[2]; Uint32 Rmask; Uint32 Gmask; Uint32 Bmask; Uint32 Amask; ...}` — more fields than this
/// module reads. Only `format`, the `SDL_PixelFormatEnum` value at offset 0, is read: it is what decides
/// whether `texture_from` must convert before uploading.
Pixel_Format_Data :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_PixelFormatEnum` value. Compare against `GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT`.
    format: u32 #place 0;
}

/// An `SDL_Surface` — pixels in ordinary memory — as a `#place` overlay of the fields worth reading.
///
/// **96 bytes**, which `layout_is_sdl2` checks. SDL allocates it, so this struct is never a local: a caller only
/// ever holds a `*Surface_Data` that SDL wrote.
///
/// The offsets are SDL2's ABI rather than a documented contract — see the module docs, which is where that
/// distinction is argued.
Surface_Data :: struct {
    /// The surface's pixel format — `SDL_PixelFormat *`. Offset 8: `flags` is a `Uint32` at offset 0, padded
    /// to 8 for this pointer, exactly the gap `width` at 16 already implied existed and nothing before this
    /// module named.
    pixel_format: *Pixel_Format_Data #place 8;
    /// Width in pixels.
    width: s32 #place 16;
    /// Height in pixels.
    height: s32 #place 20;
    /// Bytes per row, which is **not** `width * bytes_per_pixel`: SDL pads rows for alignment.
    ///
    /// Exposed because a caller who ever does read pixels needs it, and because its absence is the single most
    /// common source of a skewed image.
    pitch: s32 #place 24;
    /// The pixels. Nothing exported here reads them — see the module docs.
    pixels: *u8 #place 32;
    /// The rest of the struct, so the overlay is the 96 bytes SDL allocated.
    ///
    /// Never read. It exists so the size check below has something to check, and so no future field lands
    /// outside the struct.
    tail: [56]u8 #place 40;
}

// **This module no longer declares a `Texture`** (ADR-0182 §4). It had a one-field
// `Texture :: struct { handle: *u8; }`, and `Simp.Texture` carries `width` and `height` too — because
// Simp's does, and because every caller sizing a quad needs them. Two texture types would mean a caller
// converting between them at every boundary, so this module produces `Simp.Texture` and there is one.
//
// `Simp.Texture.handle` is a **GL texture name** (`u32`), not an SDL texture pointer — `Simp`'s only backend
// is OpenGL now, so this module's job is to get decoded pixels into a `glTexImage2D` call, not an
// `SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface` one.

/// A loaded image's pixels, before it becomes a texture.
Surface :: struct {
    /// The `SDL_Surface *`. Null when the surface is not loaded.
    handle: *Surface_Data;
}

/// `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA32`'s value on a little-endian host (`SDL_PIXELFORMAT_ABGR8888`) — the format this
/// module creates surfaces in and the one every upload to GL converts to first. See the module docs for why
/// the literal `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888` is the wrong constant here.
///
/// The value rather than the macro, since a `#foreign` declaration cannot name a macro and the header computes
/// this from a bitfield, and rather than `SDL_PIXELFORMAT_RGBA8888`'s formula for the reason above. Checked
/// against the C value rather than transcribed from either formula.
GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT :: 376840196;

/// Whether `Surface_Data` is the 96 bytes SDL2 allocates.
///
/// **A file-scope constant at last** (ADR-0180 §3). It was a procedure for the reason ADR-0165 §7
/// recorded: `size_of` of a struct was not reachable from file scope (E0230), and `Socket` and `Window`
/// had both moved the same check into one.
///
/// **Named `SURFACE_LAYOUT_IS_SDL2` and not `LAYOUT_IS_SDL2`**, which is what its procedure form was
/// first called: `Window` has one too, and a file importing both got **E0211, ambiguous name** — the
/// flat-namespace hazard ADR-0166 §7 recorded, arriving for real one wave later. ADR-0179 makes a
/// qualified import the answer, and the prefix stays anyway: a module's exported name is a claim on
/// every importer that spells it bare.
SURFACE_LAYOUT_IS_SDL2 :: size_of(Surface_Data) == 96;

/// Loads a BMP file into a surface.
///
/// `path` must be NUL-terminated — a `*u8`, not a `string`, for `Window.create_window`'s reason.
///
/// `#must`: a missing or corrupt file is the *expected* failure of this routine, and it is the reason ADR-0166 §
/// rejected deferring images entirely. A caller who ignores the flag gets a null handle and a null texture and
/// draws nothing, with no clue which of the three steps failed.
load_bmp :: (path: *u8) -> (Surface, bool) #must {
    s: Surface;
    mode := "rb\0";
    source := sdl_rw_from_file(path, mode.data);
    if source == null {
        s.handle = null;
        return s, false;
    }
    // `1` frees the `SDL_RWops` whether the decode succeeds or fails, which is why there is no leak on the
    // failure path below and no second cleanup call anywhere in this module.
    s.handle = sdl_load_bmp_rw(source, 1);
    return s, s.handle != null;
}

/// Creates an empty RGBA surface `width` by `height`.
///
/// Here because the *test* for this module needs one: an image can then be built, saved and loaded back, so
/// nothing binary lives in the repository. A caller building an image procedurally wants it too.
create_surface :: (width: s64, height: s64) -> (Surface, bool) #must {
    s: Surface;
    s.handle = sdl_create_surface(0, cast(s32, width), cast(s32, height), 32, cast(u32, GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT));
    return s, s.handle != null;
}

/// Fills `area` of `surface` with a packed 32-bit colour.
///
/// `colour` is written to memory exactly as given — `SDL_FillRect` does not reinterpret it through the
/// surface's format — so on this (little-endian) host `0xRRGGBBAA` lands as bytes `R, G, B, A` in that
/// order, which is `GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT`'s own byte order: for a `create_surface` surface, `0xFF0000FF` is
/// opaque red. A caller mixing this with a surface loaded from a file must know that file's format, which
/// is the honest reason this module has no `set_pixel`.
fill_surface :: (surface: *Surface, area: *Window.Rect, colour: s64) -> bool #must {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    // Narrowed at the boundary: SDL's `Uint32` is the surface's packed pixel, and a `s64` caller-facing
    // parameter is what lets `0xFF0000FF` be written without a cast at every call site.
    return sdl_fill_surface(surface.handle, area, cast(u32, colour)) == 0;
}

/// Writes `surface` to `path` as a BMP.
///
/// `#must`: a write can fail for every ordinary reason, and a caller who ignores it and then loads the file back
/// gets the *load*'s failure instead, one step removed from the cause.
save_bmp :: (surface: *Surface, path: *u8) -> bool #must {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return false;
    }
    mode := "wb\0";
    destination := sdl_rw_from_file(path, mode.data);
    if destination == null {
        return false;
    }
    return sdl_save_bmp_rw(surface.handle, destination, 1) == 0;
}

/// The surface's width in pixels, or 0 when it is not loaded.
width_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.width);
}

/// The surface's height in pixels, or 0 when it is not loaded.
height_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.height);
}

/// The surface's row stride in bytes, or 0 when it is not loaded.
///
/// **Not** `width * 4`: SDL pads rows, and assuming otherwise is what skews an image.
pitch_of :: (surface: *Surface) -> s64 {
    if surface.handle == null {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(s64, surface.handle.pitch);
}

/// Frees `surface` and marks it freed. Safe on an already-freed one.
free_surface :: (surface: *Surface) {
    if surface.handle != null {
        sdl_free_surface(surface.handle);
    }
    surface.handle = null;
}

/// Uploads `surface` to a GL texture `Simp` can draw from.
///
/// The surface stays valid and is the caller's to `free_surface`: this reads out of it and never frees it,
/// so `load_bmp` plus `texture_from` stays usable for a caller who wanted both.
///
/// Needs a GL context already current — see the module docs. Converts to `GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT` first when the
/// surface is not already in it, and repacks a padded row into temporary storage when a converted surface's
/// `pitch` still is not `width * 4` — see the module docs for why both exist and why `GL.set_unpack_alignment`
/// does not.
texture_from :: (surface: *Surface) -> (Simp.Texture, bool) #must {
    t: Simp.Texture;
    t.handle = 0;
    t.width = 0;
    t.height = 0;
    if surface.handle == null {
        return t, false;
    }

    // Step 1: make sure the pixels are RGBA8888. `converted` is this procedure's own temporary — never the
    // caller's surface — and is freed before returning on every path once it exists.
    working := surface.handle;
    converted: *Surface_Data = null;
    if working.pixel_format == null || working.pixel_format.format != cast(u32, GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT) {
        converted = sdl_convert_surface_format(working, cast(u32, GL_UPLOAD_FORMAT), 0);
        if converted == null {
            return t, false;
        }
        working = converted;
    }

    width := cast(s64, working.width);
    height := cast(s64, working.height);
    pitch := cast(s64, working.pitch);
    tight_pitch := width * 4;

    // Step 2: get a tightly-packed RGBA buffer. Already tight is the common case — measured, see the module
    // docs — so the repack only runs for a surface this module did not build itself.
    pixels := working.pixels;
    if pitch != tight_pitch {
        scratch := talloc(tight_pitch * height);
        if scratch == null {
            if converted != null {
                sdl_free_surface(converted);
            }
            return t, false;
        }
        source := view(working.pixels, pitch * height);
        dest := view(scratch, tight_pitch * height);
        row := 0;
        while row < height {
            column := 0;
            while column < tight_pitch {
                dest[row * tight_pitch + column] = source[row * pitch + column];
                column = column + 1;
            }
            row = row + 1;
        }
        pixels = scratch;
    }

    // Step 3: upload. `GL.set_unpack_alignment` is not called — `pixels` is tightly-packed RGBA by this point
    // on every path, and `width * 4` is always a multiple of GL's default 4-byte alignment, so the knob that
    // exists for a 3-channel row has nothing to correct here.
    handle := GL.create_texture();
    if handle == 0 {
        if converted != null {
            sdl_free_surface(converted);
        }
        return t, false;
    }
    GL.bind_texture_unit_0(handle);
    GL.upload_texture_rgba(width, height, pixels);

    if converted != null {
        sdl_free_surface(converted);
    }

    t.handle = handle;
    t.width = width;
    t.height = height;
    return t, true;
}

/// Loads a BMP straight to a texture, freeing the intermediate surface.
///
/// The common case in one call. It frees the surface **on both paths**, including when the upload fails, which
/// is the leak a caller writing this sequence by hand gets wrong — the failure path is the one nobody runs.
///
/// Needs a GL context already current — see the module docs.
load_texture :: (path: *u8) -> (Simp.Texture, bool) #must {
    t: Simp.Texture;
    s, ok := load_bmp(path);
    if !ok {
        t.handle = 0;
        t.width = 0;
        t.height = 0;
        return t, false;
    }
    t, uploaded := texture_from(*s);
    free_surface(*s);
    return t, uploaded;
}

/// Destroys `texture` and marks it destroyed. Safe on an already-destroyed one.
///
/// Deletes the **GL** texture (`GL.delete_texture`, which is itself safe on the zero handle a destroyed or
/// never-loaded texture carries) — there is no SDL texture any more to destroy instead.
destroy_texture :: (texture: *Simp.Texture) {
    GL.delete_texture(texture.handle);
    texture.handle = 0;
    texture.width = 0;
    texture.height = 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Private: the raw SDL2 bindings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Not exported (ADR-0054 §2, ADR-0179 §6). A caller wants `load_bmp`, not `sdl_load_bmp_rw`; this
// file had no `#scope_module`, so every raw symbol reached every importer's flat namespace.
#scope_module

/// The SDL2 shared library. Named again here rather than re-exported, because a `#foreign` declaration resolves
/// its library in **its own file** (ADR-0019 §4) and a name imported from `Window` would not be that.
sdl :: #system_library "SDL2";

/// `SDL_RWFromFile(const char *file, const char *mode)`.
sdl_rw_from_file :: (path: *u8, mode: *u8) -> *u8 #foreign sdl "SDL_RWFromFile";

/// `SDL_LoadBMP_RW(SDL_RWops *, int freesrc)`.
///
/// `SDL_LoadBMP` is a **macro** in the header, not a symbol, so this is what a `#foreign` declaration can name.
sdl_load_bmp_rw :: (source: *u8, free_source: s32) -> *Surface_Data #foreign sdl "SDL_LoadBMP_RW";

/// `SDL_SaveBMP_RW(SDL_Surface *, SDL_RWops *, int freedst)`.
sdl_save_bmp_rw :: (surface: *Surface_Data, destination: *u8, free_destination: s32) -> s32 #foreign sdl "SDL_SaveBMP_RW";

/// `SDL_CreateRGBSurfaceWithFormat(Uint32 flags, int w, int h, int depth, Uint32 format)`.
sdl_create_surface :: (flags: u32, width: s32, height: s32, depth: s32, format: u32) -> *Surface_Data #foreign sdl "SDL_CreateRGBSurfaceWithFormat";

/// `SDL_FreeSurface`.
sdl_free_surface :: (surface: *Surface_Data) #foreign sdl "SDL_FreeSurface";

/// `SDL_FillRect(SDL_Surface *, const SDL_Rect *, Uint32 color)`.
sdl_fill_surface :: (surface: *Surface_Data, area: *Window.Rect, colour: u32) -> s32 #foreign sdl "SDL_FillRect";

/// `SDL_ConvertSurfaceFormat(SDL_Surface *src, Uint32 pixel_format, Uint32 flags)`.
///
/// `flags` is unused by SDL today and must be `0` — the header says so. Returns a **new** surface the caller
/// (here, `texture_from`) owns and must free; the source surface is untouched, which is what lets `texture_from`
/// free only the surface it created and never the caller's.
sdl_convert_surface_format :: (surface: *Surface_Data, format: u32, flags: u32) -> *Surface_Data #foreign sdl "SDL_ConvertSurfaceFormat";
